1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,420
In 1985, engineers in
Britain switched on a

2
00:00:03,650 --> 00:00:06,280
brand-new processor for
the very first time.

3
00:00:06,637 --> 00:00:09,237
It worked. Which was
already a small miracle.

4
00:00:09,797 --> 00:00:12,077
But then they noticed something strange.

5
00:00:12,877 --> 00:00:15,197
The chip's power supply wasn't connected.

6
00:00:15,554 --> 00:00:17,094
And yet it was running.

7
00:00:17,690 --> 00:00:20,230
It was getting by on tiny
currents leaking in through

8
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:24,090
its input pins, from the
signals of the chips around it.

9
00:00:24,413 --> 00:00:28,773
When they measured it properly, the
processor needed about a tenth of a watt.

10
00:00:29,215 --> 00:00:31,135
Nobody had aimed for that.

11
00:00:31,635 --> 00:00:34,675
The engineers just wanted a
chip that was cheap to make.

12
00:00:35,029 --> 00:00:37,469
That chip was the first ARM processor.

13
00:00:38,704 --> 00:00:42,644
Forty years later, its descendants
are in almost every smartphone.

14
00:00:43,404 --> 00:00:48,424
In Apple's computers. In some of the
largest data centres in the world.

15
00:00:49,023 --> 00:00:50,903
Hundreds of billions of chips.

16
00:00:51,503 --> 00:00:56,263
So how did a processor from a British
home-computer company take over computing?

17
00:00:57,060 --> 00:01:00,300
The answer isn't simply that
it used very little power.

18
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:02,960
That part was almost an accident.

19
00:01:03,729 --> 00:01:07,309
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

20
00:01:07,609 --> 00:01:09,609
It really helps the channel.

21
00:01:10,648 --> 00:01:15,008
To understand what really happened,
we have to go back to 1983.

22
00:01:16,171 --> 00:01:18,391
The company was Acorn Computers.

23
00:01:19,251 --> 00:01:22,631
Its BBC Micro was in
classrooms all over Britain.

24
00:01:23,309 --> 00:01:28,249
The BBC Micro ran on an older,
eight-bit chip, the 6502.

25
00:01:28,509 --> 00:01:32,249
For its next machine, Acorn
needed something much faster.

26
00:01:32,728 --> 00:01:35,848
So the team tested the new
processors on the market.

27
00:01:36,588 --> 00:01:38,888
None of them did what Acorn wanted.

28
00:01:39,431 --> 00:01:44,211
They were complicated, and none of them
really fit what Acorn wanted to build.

29
00:01:44,682 --> 00:01:49,042
Then two Acorn engineers, Sophie
Wilson and Steve Furber, went to

30
00:01:49,272 --> 00:01:54,402
Arizona, to a firm started by one
of the 6502's original designers.

31
00:01:55,231 --> 00:01:57,411
They expected a big chip company.

32
00:01:58,131 --> 00:02:01,971
Instead, they found a handful of
people working out of a suburban house.

33
00:02:02,489 --> 00:02:04,909
And they came home with
a simple conclusion.

34
00:02:05,469 --> 00:02:08,589
If they can design a
processor... so can we.

35
00:02:10,929 --> 00:02:15,029
At the time, processors kept gaining
more and more elaborate instructions.

36
00:02:15,889 --> 00:02:18,689
A single instruction could
fetch a number from memory,

37
00:02:18,769 --> 00:02:21,989
do arithmetic with it,
and write the result back.

38
00:02:22,663 --> 00:02:24,463
Convenient for the programmer.

39
00:02:24,783 --> 00:02:28,543
But every instruction like that
makes the chip harder to build.

40
00:02:29,246 --> 00:02:34,426
It needs more logic just to work out
what each one means. More transistors.

41
00:02:34,906 --> 00:02:36,166
More design time.

42
00:02:37,277 --> 00:02:41,457
Researchers at Berkeley and Stanford
had asked a radical question.

43
00:02:42,173 --> 00:02:43,953
What if a processor only had simple

44
00:02:44,183 --> 00:02:47,013
instructions, and the
compiler did the rest?

45
00:02:47,687 --> 00:02:49,547
They called it RISC.

46
00:02:49,827 --> 00:02:51,767
Reduced Instruction Set Computer.

47
00:02:52,807 --> 00:02:56,707
Sophie Wilson designed ARM's
instructions along those lines.

48
00:02:57,404 --> 00:02:59,324
Arithmetic only works on the chip's own

49
00:02:59,554 --> 00:03:03,284
registers, small, fast
slots inside the processor.

50
00:03:03,804 --> 00:03:06,104
Memory gets its own instructions.

51
00:03:06,653 --> 00:03:08,953
So adding a number from
memory becomes three steps.

52
00:03:09,183 --> 00:03:12,913
Load it. Add it. Store it.

53
00:03:13,209 --> 00:03:15,429
It's called a load-store architecture.

54
00:03:16,291 --> 00:03:17,991
More steps for the program.

55
00:03:18,711 --> 00:03:21,951
But each step is regular,
predictable, and easy to decode.

56
00:03:22,589 --> 00:03:25,309
And ARM had plenty of
registers to work with.

57
00:03:25,809 --> 00:03:27,769
Sixteen, each thirty-two bits wide.

58
00:03:27,999 --> 00:03:31,909
The 6502 had three small ones.

59
00:03:34,279 --> 00:03:35,699
The result was the ARM1.

60
00:03:36,671 --> 00:03:39,431
It had about 25,000 transistors.

61
00:03:40,223 --> 00:03:44,543
Intel's 286, launched a few
years earlier, had about 134,000.

62
00:03:46,826 --> 00:03:50,966
Fewer transistors meant a
smaller chip. A cheaper chip.

63
00:03:51,260 --> 00:03:55,120
And, almost as a side effect, a
chip that barely used any power.

64
00:03:55,793 --> 00:03:58,133
That's where the tenth
of a watt came from.

65
00:03:58,833 --> 00:04:02,593
Acorn wanted a cheap plastic package,
which meant staying under one watt.

66
00:04:02,673 --> 00:04:04,333
So they designed carefully...

67
00:04:04,563 --> 00:04:06,973
and landed ten times below.

68
00:04:09,583 --> 00:04:16,083
In 1987, a faster version of the ARM went
into Acorn's new computer, the Archimedes.

69
00:04:16,805 --> 00:04:20,225
It was one of the fastest
personal computers of its day.

70
00:04:21,026 --> 00:04:22,486
And it barely mattered.

71
00:04:23,556 --> 00:04:27,876
The world was buying IBM-compatible
PCs with Intel processors.

72
00:04:28,496 --> 00:04:30,576
That's where the software was.

73
00:04:31,116 --> 00:04:32,756
That's what businesses bought.

74
00:04:33,491 --> 00:04:36,271
The Archimedes stayed
mostly in British schools.

75
00:04:36,864 --> 00:04:39,004
And Acorn itself was struggling.

76
00:04:39,804 --> 00:04:43,704
It had already needed rescuing
by Olivetti, back in 1985.

77
00:04:46,170 --> 00:04:47,950
Then help came from Apple.

78
00:04:48,590 --> 00:04:51,450
It was building a handheld
computer called the Newton.

79
00:04:52,220 --> 00:04:56,160
It needed a processor that was fast,
cheap, and could run on batteries.

80
00:04:57,031 --> 00:04:59,011
The ARM fitted perfectly.

81
00:04:59,311 --> 00:05:03,591
But Apple didn't want to depend on a
chip owned by a rival computer maker.

82
00:05:04,082 --> 00:05:09,462
So in 1990, the ARM team was spun out
of Acorn into a company of its own.

83
00:05:10,002 --> 00:05:14,042
Acorn, Apple and the chip
maker VLSI owned it together.

84
00:05:14,357 --> 00:05:17,217
Twelve engineers. An
old barn near Cambridge.

85
00:05:17,715 --> 00:05:20,935
And a business model that turned
out to be the real secret.

86
00:05:21,673 --> 00:05:26,633
Intel designed its processors, made them
in its own factories, and sold them.

87
00:05:27,145 --> 00:05:30,885
ARM would make nothing at
all. It would sell the design.

88
00:05:31,637 --> 00:05:34,637
Any company could license
it, build the ARM core into

89
00:05:34,867 --> 00:05:38,677
its own chip, and pay a small
fee on every chip it sold.

90
00:05:39,092 --> 00:05:42,532
That mattered, because a chip
doesn't have to be just a processor.

91
00:05:43,192 --> 00:05:48,192
Around the ARM core you can put a radio,
a graphics unit, a camera processor.

92
00:05:48,918 --> 00:05:52,918
One chip, designed by whoever
needs it, with ARM at the centre.

93
00:05:54,001 --> 00:05:57,201
ARM didn't have to beat
Intel in personal computers.

94
00:05:57,961 --> 00:06:00,001
It could end up inside anything.

95
00:06:00,369 --> 00:06:04,729
And in the 1990s, anything
increasingly meant mobile phones.

96
00:06:05,415 --> 00:06:08,975
A desktop computer can use a
hundred watts and a big fan.

97
00:06:09,715 --> 00:06:12,095
A phone runs on a battery in your pocket.

98
00:06:12,787 --> 00:06:15,627
By the end of the decade,
ARM cores were inside some

99
00:06:15,857 --> 00:06:18,907
of the best-selling phones,
from Nokia and others.

100
00:06:19,382 --> 00:06:22,622
Then came the iPod. Then the smartphone.

101
00:06:23,427 --> 00:06:25,747
Billions of people
suddenly carried a computer

102
00:06:25,977 --> 00:06:28,407
that had to last all day on a battery.

103
00:06:28,791 --> 00:06:31,471
And the processor that was
built because it was cheap

104
00:06:31,551 --> 00:06:34,311
turned out to be exactly
what that world needed.

105
00:06:35,324 --> 00:06:37,044
So that's our answer, right?

106
00:06:37,684 --> 00:06:41,104
ARM is simple, simple means
efficient, and efficient wins.

107
00:06:42,043 --> 00:06:43,243
Not quite.

108
00:06:45,901 --> 00:06:48,681
Look inside a modern high-end ARM core.

109
00:06:49,261 --> 00:06:52,481
Huge caches. Several
instructions running at once.

110
00:06:52,711 --> 00:06:58,241
Branch prediction, guessing which way
the program will go before it gets there.

111
00:06:59,227 --> 00:07:03,507
Out-of-order execution, running
instructions in whatever order is fastest.

112
00:07:04,347 --> 00:07:06,747
Billions of transistors on the chip.

113
00:07:07,227 --> 00:07:09,067
Nothing about it is simple.

114
00:07:09,621 --> 00:07:11,821
So where did the simplicity go?

115
00:07:13,133 --> 00:07:16,653
The trick is that a processor
is really two different things.

116
00:07:17,389 --> 00:07:20,649
The instruction set is the
language the software speaks.

117
00:07:20,989 --> 00:07:24,129
Which instructions exist,
and what they mean.

118
00:07:24,719 --> 00:07:28,759
The microarchitecture is the machine
that actually carries them out.

119
00:07:29,338 --> 00:07:31,798
The language can stay
simple, while the machine

120
00:07:32,028 --> 00:07:34,578
underneath becomes enormously complicated.

121
00:07:36,029 --> 00:07:38,709
And the same thing
happened on the other side.

122
00:07:39,367 --> 00:07:43,527
Intel's x86 instructions
are famously complicated.

123
00:07:44,049 --> 00:07:47,789
But since the mid-1990s,
Intel and AMD processors

124
00:07:48,019 --> 00:07:51,409
break them down inside into
small, simple operations.

125
00:07:51,979 --> 00:07:56,739
Then they run those on an engine much
like the one inside a modern ARM core.

126
00:07:57,302 --> 00:08:00,842
Underneath the labels, the two
have grown surprisingly alike.

127
00:08:02,158 --> 00:08:04,078
ARM's cleaner instructions still help.

128
00:08:04,308 --> 00:08:08,138
They're easier to decode,
which can save a little power.

129
00:08:08,469 --> 00:08:12,849
But they're not a magic advantage
that x86 could never match.

130
00:08:14,136 --> 00:08:16,276
So ARM didn't take over
because it was simple.

131
00:08:16,506 --> 00:08:19,536
And it wasn't designed for low power.

132
00:08:20,293 --> 00:08:24,613
It won because three things
lined up. An efficient design.

133
00:08:24,893 --> 00:08:27,813
A business model that
let anyone build with it.

134
00:08:28,213 --> 00:08:31,973
And a world that suddenly needed
billions of battery-powered computers.

135
00:08:34,743 --> 00:08:38,283
And then, in 2020, the
story came back to Apple.

136
00:08:38,977 --> 00:08:43,777
Apple had sold its stake in ARM years
earlier, when it badly needed the money.

137
00:08:44,364 --> 00:08:47,304
Now it put its own ARM-based
chips into the Mac.

138
00:08:48,001 --> 00:08:51,781
The same kind of cores as the
iPhone, scaled up for a computer.

139
00:08:52,621 --> 00:08:56,981
Fast cores, efficient cores, graphics
and much more, in a single chip.

140
00:08:57,593 --> 00:09:00,393
They were faster than the
Intel chips they replaced,

141
00:09:00,633 --> 00:09:03,193
and the laptops ran
far longer on a charge.

142
00:09:03,939 --> 00:09:06,659
ARM was no longer just
the phone processor.

143
00:09:07,585 --> 00:09:09,305
And ARM didn't stop there.

144
00:09:09,745 --> 00:09:13,345
It moved into data centres too,
where every watt costs money.

145
00:09:13,945 --> 00:09:17,025
Amazon now designs its
own ARM server chips.

146
00:09:19,275 --> 00:09:25,075
The first ARM chip had about 25,000
transistors. Today's have billions.

147
00:09:25,965 --> 00:09:29,325
Almost everything about how
they work inside has changed.

148
00:09:30,735 --> 00:09:34,175
A small team wanted a processor
they could actually build.

149
00:09:34,842 --> 00:09:38,382
They didn't build it for phones,
for Apple, or to beat Intel.

150
00:09:39,234 --> 00:09:42,894
And in the end, the most important
thing they made wasn't the chip.

151
00:09:43,511 --> 00:09:46,801
It was a simple foundation that
everyone else could build on.
